    "articles": [
      {
        "no": 1,
        "text": {
          "en": "Where a debtor is unable to pay off debts due and any of the following circumstances exists, the people's court shall determine that the debtor has grounds for bankruptcy:\n(a) its assets are insufficient to pay off all its debts;\n(b) it clearly lacks the capacity to pay off debts.\nWhere a party concerned, on the ground that a person jointly and severally liable for the debts of the debtor has not lost the capacity to pay off debts, asserts that the debtor does not have grounds for bankruptcy, the people's court shall not uphold the assertion."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 1."
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 2,
        "text": {
          "en": "Where the following circumstances exist simultaneously, the people's court shall determine that the debtor is unable to pay off debts due:\n(a) the creditor-debtor relationship is established in accordance with the law;\n(b) the time limit for performance of the debt has expired;\n(c) the debtor has not paid off the debt in full."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 2."
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 3,
        "text": {
          "en": "Where the balance sheet of the debtor, or an audit report or an asset valuation report, shows that all its assets are insufficient to pay off all its liabilities, the people's court shall determine that the assets of the debtor are insufficient to pay off all its debts, unless there is contrary evidence sufficient to prove that the assets of the debtor are capable of paying off all its liabilities."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 3."
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 4,
        "text": {
          "en": "Where the book assets of the debtor are greater than its liabilities but any of the following circumstances exists, the people's court shall determine that the debtor clearly lacks the capacity to pay off debts:\n(a) it is unable to pay off debts due to a severe shortage of funds, the non-realisation of property or other reasons;\n(b) the legal representative is missing and no other person is responsible for managing the property, so that the debts cannot be paid off;\n(c) the debts cannot be paid off after compulsory enforcement by a people's court;\n(d) the debtor has suffered losses for a long period and has difficulty in turning the business around, so that the debts cannot be paid off;\n(e) other circumstances causing the debtor to lose the capacity to pay off debts."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 4."
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 5,
        "text": {
          "en": "Where an enterprise legal person has been dissolved but has not been liquidated, or has not completed liquidation within a reasonable period, and a creditor applies for bankruptcy liquidation of the debtor, the people's court shall accept the application, unless the debtor proves within the statutory period for objection that no grounds for bankruptcy have arisen."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 5."
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 6,
        "text": {
          "en": "Where a creditor applies for bankruptcy of a debtor, it shall submit the relevant evidence that the debtor is unable to pay off debts due. Where the debtor fails to raise an objection to the application of the creditor with the people's court within the statutory time limit, or the objection is not established, the people's court shall rule in accordance with the law to accept the bankruptcy application.\nAfter accepting a bankruptcy application, the people's court shall order the debtor to submit in accordance with the law such relevant materials as a statement of its property status, a list of debts, a list of claims and financial accounting reports; where the debtor refuses to submit them, the people's court may take compulsory measures such as a fine against the person directly responsible of the debtor."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 6."
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 7,
        "text": {
          "en": "When a people's court receives a bankruptcy application, it shall issue to the applicant a written receipt for the application and the evidence attached thereto.\nAfter receiving a bankruptcy application, the people's court shall promptly review the standing of the applicant, the standing of the debtor and the grounds for bankruptcy, as well as the relevant materials and evidence, and shall rule in accordance with Article 10 of the Enterprise Bankruptcy Law on whether to accept the application.\nWhere the people's court considers that the applicant should supplement or correct the relevant materials, it shall notify the applicant within five days from the date of receipt of the bankruptcy application. The period during which a party supplements or corrects the relevant materials shall not be counted in the time limit provided in Article 10 of the Enterprise Bankruptcy Law."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 7."
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 8,
        "text": {
          "en": "The litigation costs of a bankruptcy case shall, in accordance with Article 43 of the Enterprise Bankruptcy Law, be paid out of the property of the debtor. Where a party concerned objects to the bankruptcy application on the ground that the applicant has not paid the litigation costs in advance, the people's court shall not uphold the objection."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 8."
        }
      },
      {
        "no": 9,
        "text": {
          "en": "Where an applicant files a bankruptcy application with a people's court and the people's court does not accept the application, or does not act in accordance with Article 7 of these Provisions, the applicant may file a bankruptcy application with the people's court at the next higher level.\nAfter receiving the bankruptcy application, the people's court at the next higher level shall order the lower court to review it in accordance with the law and promptly rule on whether to accept it; where the lower court still fails to rule on whether to accept it, the people's court at the next higher level may itself make the ruling.\nWhere the people's court at the next higher level rules to accept the bankruptcy application, it may at the same time direct the lower people's court to try the case."
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 9."
        }
      }
    ]
